    <span class="kicker">How it computes</span>
    <h2>Read top-down, calculate bottom-up</h2>
    <p class="lede">You read the tree top-down to understand it, but numbers flow <b>upward like sap</b>. You supply the Level-5 raw data points; each score combines its children, feeds its parent, and so on up to the domain score and finally the composite.</p>
    <div class="card" style="margin-top:18px">
      <div class="lbl" style="font-size:.71rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.09em;color:var(--muted);font-weight:700;margin-bottom:10px">A worked example — the carbon-footprint branch</div>
      <div id="ladder"></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- STRATEGIES -->
<section id="strategies">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker">The engine</span>
    <h2 id="stratHeading">Calculation strategies</h2>
    <p class="lede">Every row's <em>Calculation Strategy</em> says how its value is produced. Leaves are raw values; above them a row is either a <b>score</b> (always 0–1) or a <b>raw total</b> (a sum or formula in kg, €, …). The number on each card is how many indicators use that strategy.</p>
    <div class="grid g3" id="stratGrid" style="margin-top:18px"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WEIGHTS & NORMALIZATION -->
<section id="weights">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker">Per-company settings</span>
    <h2>Weights &amp; normalization</h2>
    <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:14px">
      <div>
        <h3>Weights sum to 1</h3>
        <p>When a score combines several children, a <b>weight</b> says how much each child counts. Under any one parent, the weights of all direct children should add up to <b>1</b>, keeping every score on the same 0–1 scale. The template ships <b>equal 1/N defaults</b>; each company overrides them. If a whole branch has no data, its weight is effectively treated as 0 so it drops out rather than dragging the score down.</p>
        <div class="callout info" style="margin-top:6px"><b>1 = good, 0 = bad</b> everywhere. Scores are shown as a percentage of that 0–1 fraction.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Normalization &amp; direction</h3>
        <p>Raw values are mapped to 0–1 by min-max normalization: <code>(Value − Min) / (Max − Min)</code>. There is <b>no separate higher/lower switch</b> — direction is decided entirely by which bound is larger.</p>
        <div class="normbox" style="margin-top:12px">
          <div class="band">
            <h4>Higher is better</h4>
            <div style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--muted)">shares, ratings, efficiency</div>
            <div class="gradient up"></div>
            <div class="bandlbl"><span>Min&nbsp;=&nbsp;worst</span><span>Max&nbsp;=&nbsp;best</span></div>
            <div style="font-size:.8rem;margin-top:6px">Min &lt; Max (ascending band)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="band">
            <h4>Lower is better</h4>
            <div style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--muted)">intensity, time, cost</div>
            <div class="gradient down"></div>
            <div class="bandlbl"><span>Min&nbsp;=&nbsp;worst</span><span>Max&nbsp;=&nbsp;best</span></div>
            <div style="font-size:.8rem;margin-top:6px"><b>Invert:</b> set Min &gt; Max</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout" style="margin-top:12px"><b>Mind the direction.</b> For e.g. energy-per-unit where 25 kWh is worst and 0 is ideal, set <b>Min&nbsp;=&nbsp;25, Max&nbsp;=&nbsp;0</b>. A 5 kWh product then scores 0.8. An ordinary ascending band would score it backwards.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WORKFLOW -->
<section id="workflow">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker">For practitioners</span>
    <h2>The recommended workflow</h2>
    <p class="lede">The key sequence: <b>understand top-down → gather data first → set weights and min/max afterwards.</b></p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><b>Read the Overview &amp; Top-Level sheets.</b><span>Orient yourself: the goal, the structure, the R-principles, and what each of the five domains means.</span></li>
      <li><b>Pick a domain and drill down, top-down.</b><span>Read from the Level-1 score down to the Level-5 raw data points to see why each input is needed and where it fits.</span></li>
      <li><b>Identify the raw inputs you must supply.</b><span>They are the grey <code>Data? = x</code> rows. The Metrics List sheet is the flat checklist of all of them, with links back to context.</span></li>
      <li><b>Gather the data first.</b><span>Use each row's Unit, Data Source (often LCA) and Life-Cycle Stages to know what to measure — before worrying about weights.</span></li>
      <li><b>Then set the per-company values.</b><span>Fill in Weights (sum to 1 under each parent) and the Target Min / Target Max reference values. Mind the direction for lower-is-better metrics.</span></li>
      <li><b>Consult the References as needed.</b><span>For any KPI whose basis you want to verify, follow its Reference code to the References sheet.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

